#---
# purpose: "several binary literals over the shared-binary threshold can all be matched, and the program still exits cleanly"
# oracle: oracle.exs
#---
#
# # binary_literal_over_threshold
#
# A binary literal above `SHARED_BIN_THRESHOLD_BYTES` (64) is compiled into a
# static shared binary baked into the program's own data. Matching MORE THAN ONE
# of them printed the right answers and then died at teardown with SIGBUS, on
# `run` only, silently -- exit 138 after a clean-looking run.
#
#     one big literal, matched         ->  exit 0
#     TWO big literals, both matched   ->  exit 138
#
# `TAG_MASK` is four bits and `TAG_FWD` is `0x8`. A ProcBin stub keeps its
# SharedBin's address in word 0, and word 0 is where the collector writes its
# forwarding marker -- so a SharedBin sitting at an address ending in 8 makes a
# LIVE stub read as forwarded. The sweep then took the SharedBin itself for a
# to-space stub, wrote into it, and made the chain head point eight bytes below
# it; the release that followed did an atomic decrement into read-only memory.
#
# Two statics emitted back to back are exactly how you land there: the first is
# 16-aligned and safe, the second sits 40 bytes later and ends in 8. Half of all
# heap-allocated shared binaries were a coin flip on the same hazard.
#
# The fix is the invariant the heap already keeps -- 16-byte alignment, so the
# low four bits are never part of an address. This fixture is FOUR literals over
# the threshold, matched, so no single alignment accident can make it pass.
defp first_byte(<<c, _rest :: binary>>), do: c
defp first_byte(_b), do: :no_match

defp walk(<<_c, rest :: binary>>, n), do: walk(rest, n + 1)
defp walk(_b, n), do: n

def main() do
  dbg(first_byte("aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa"))
  dbg(first_byte("bbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbb"))
  dbg(walk("aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa", 0))
  dbg(walk("bbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbb", 0))
  dbg(walk("cccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccc", 0))
  dbg(walk("dddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddd", 0))
end
